---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Cloud Migration & Landing Zone Guide"
  description="Plan a cloud migration that does not turn into a fire drill: account structure, network design, data migration strategy, and a cutover plan with a real rollback path."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-content">
      <a class="back" href="/guides/">← Back to Guides</a>
      <span class="eyebrow">Cloud · Guide</span>
      <h1>Cloud Migration &amp; Landing Zone Guide</h1>
      <p>
        Most cloud migrations don't fail on the technical move — they fail
        on the planning that was skipped beforehand. This guide covers
        the decisions that are expensive to get wrong.
      </p>
      <div class="meta"><span>13 min read</span><span>·</span><span>Concepts apply across AWS, Azure, and GCP</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-wrap">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <h2>On this page</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#landing-zone">Start with account structure, not workloads</a></li>
        <li><a href="#network">Design the network before you migrate anything</a></li>
        <li><a href="#strategy">Choosing a migration strategy per workload</a></li>
        <li><a href="#data">Migrating data without extended downtime</a></li>
        <li><a href="#cutover">Plan the cutover with a real rollback path</a></li>
        <li><a href="#cost">Cost visibility from day one</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pitfalls">Common pitfalls that turn into incidents</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="landing-zone">Start with account structure, not workloads</h2>
    <p>
      The single most consequential early decision is account (or
      subscription/project) structure — and it's the most expensive to
      change later, because every IAM policy, network route, and
      deployment pipeline ends up referencing it. Set this up before
      migrating a single workload.
    </p>
    <p>
      A structure that scales for most organizations:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A management/root account</strong> for billing consolidation and organization-wide policy, with no workloads running in it directly.</li>
      <li><strong>A shared-services account</strong> for things every other account needs — centralized logging, identity federation, shared networking.</li>
      <li><strong>Separate accounts per environment</strong> — production, staging, development — so a mistake in one can't touch another.</li>
      <li><strong>A dedicated security/audit account</strong> that receives logs but where workload teams have no write access, so audit trails can't be tampered with by the same people whose actions are being logged.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This separation gives you real blast-radius isolation: a
      misconfigured security group in staging can't accidentally expose a
      production database, because they're not even in the same account.
    </p>

    <h2 id="network">Design the network before you migrate anything</h2>
    <p>
      Decide your IP address plan across every environment and region
      before you provision a single VPC — retrofitting a network design
      after workloads exist means re-addressing live systems, which is
      exactly the kind of change nobody wants to schedule.
    </p>
    <p>
      Specifically settle these before migrating:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>CIDR ranges per environment, with no overlap — overlapping ranges make future VPC peering or transit gateway connections impossible without re-addressing.</li>
      <li>Whether workloads need direct internet access, or should route outbound traffic through a shared NAT gateway.</li>
      <li>How on-premises networks connect in, if this is a hybrid migration — site-to-site VPN for lower traffic volumes, a dedicated private connection for sustained high throughput.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="strategy">Choosing a migration strategy per workload</h2>
    <p>
      Not every workload should be migrated the same way. Four common
      strategies, roughly in order of effort:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Rehost ("lift and shift")</strong> — move the workload as-is, usually as a VM. Fastest, but you carry over existing inefficiencies.</li>
      <li><strong>Replatform</strong> — move it with small, low-risk changes, like swapping a self-managed database for a managed one.</li>
      <li><strong>Refactor</strong> — redesign the application to use cloud-native services properly. Slowest and highest effort, but unlocks the most long-term benefit.</li>
      <li><strong>Retire</strong> — the migration itself is often the first time anyone audits what's actually running; some workloads turn out to have no real users left.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Don't force every workload into the same strategy. A legacy internal
      tool with five users is a good rehost candidate; your primary
      customer-facing database is worth the extra effort of a proper
      replatform to a managed service.
    </p>

    <h2 id="data">Migrating data without extended downtime</h2>
    <p>
      For anything beyond a small, static dataset, avoid a single
      stop-the-world copy. Use continuous replication to keep the cloud
      copy in sync with the source right up until cutover:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Start replication early, well before the planned cutover date, so there's time to validate.</li>
      <li>Continuously compare row counts, checksums, or another consistency check between source and target.</li>
      <li>At cutover, briefly freeze writes on the source, let the final delta replicate, then switch traffic.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      This turns a migration that might otherwise need a multi-hour
      maintenance window into one that needs only a few minutes of
      write-freeze at the very end.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cutover">Plan the cutover with a real rollback path</h2>
    <p>
      Write the rollback plan before the cutover, not during an incident
      caused by the cutover. A rollback plan that only exists as an
      assumption — "we could probably just point DNS back" — is not a
      plan; test it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A cutover runbook should specify, in advance:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The exact go/no-go criteria, checked at each step — not a vague "if it looks okay."</li>
      <li>Who has the authority to call a rollback, and how quickly they can be reached during the window.</li>
      <li>The specific rollback steps, already tested at least once in a rehearsal.</li>
      <li>A communication plan for stakeholders, so a rollback doesn't come as a surprise to people who need to know.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="cost">Cost visibility from day one</h2>
    <p>
      Tag every resource with owner, environment, and cost center from the
      very first deployment — retrofitting tags onto an already-large
      cloud footprint is a slow, unrewarding cleanup project that never
      quite gets prioritized. Enforce required tags through policy so
      untagged resources are rejected at creation, not caught in a
      quarterly audit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Set a cost alert before you migrate your first meaningful workload,
      not after the first surprising bill. Cloud costs can grow quickly
      from autoscaling misconfiguration, forgotten snapshots, or data
      egress that wasn't accounted for in the original estimate.
    </p>

    <h2 id="pitfalls">Common pitfalls that turn into incidents</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Broad IAM permissions "to unblock the migration faster."</strong> These rarely get tightened afterward — scope access from the start.</li>
      <li><strong>Treating a backup as a disaster recovery plan.</strong> A backup is one component; you also need a tested restore process, a defined RTO and RPO, and a plan for recovering network and DNS configuration, not just data.</li>
      <li><strong>No rehearsal of the actual cutover.</strong> The first real run of a runbook should not be the one that matters most.</li>
      <li><strong>Underestimating egress costs</strong> for workloads that talk heavily to on-premises systems or another cloud provider during a transition period.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="related-tools">
      <h2>Related tools on this site</h2>
      <p>Put this guide into practice with these free tools:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/cloud/">Cloud Tools</a></li>
        <li><a href="/network/cidr-calculator">CIDR Calculator</a></li>
        <li><a href="/interview-qa/#questions">Cloud Scenario Interview Q&amp;A</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .container { width: min(calc(100% - 32px), 820px); margin-inline: auto; }
  .hero { position: relative; overflow: hidden; padding: 56px 0 46px; background: radial-gradient(circle at 85% 15%, rgba(56, 189, 248, .2), transparent 26%), linear-gradient(135deg, #0b1220, #111d33 62%, #162a4f); color: #fff; }
  .hero:after { content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 0; pointer-events: none; background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(255, 255, 255, .035) 1px, transparent 1px), linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, .035) 1px, transparent 1px); background-size: 38px 38px; }
  .hero-content { position: relative; z-index: 1; }
  .back { display: block; margin-bottom: 18px; color: #9fb2cc; font-weight: 700; text-decoration: none; font-size: .92rem; }
  .back:hover { color: #fff; }
  .eyebrow { display: inline-block; color: #66d3ff; font-size: .76rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: .13em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .hero h1 { margin: 12px 0 16px; font-size: clamp(2rem, 4.2vw, 2.9rem); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -.03em; }
  .hero p { margin: 0; max-width: 700px; color: #c8d5e7; font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.8; }
  .meta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px; margin-top: 18px; color: #9fb2cc; font-size: .87rem; font-weight: 700; }

  .article-wrap { width: min(calc(100% - 32px), 760px); margin: 0 auto; padding: 48px 0 90px; }
  .toc { margin: 0 0 40px; padding: 22px 24px; border: 1px solid #dbe4f0; border-radius: 14px; background: #f6f9fd; }
  .toc h2 { margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: .82rem; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #2563eb; }
  .toc ol { margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; }
  .toc li { margin-bottom: 7px; }
  .toc a { color: #334155; font-weight: 650; text-decoration: none; }
  .toc a:hover { color: #2563eb; }

  .article-wrap h2 { margin: 44px 0 14px; color: #111827; font-size: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.3; scroll-margin-top: 90px; }
  .article-wrap h2:first-of-type { margin-top: 0; }
  .article-wrap p { margin: 0 0 16px; color: #374151; font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.85; }
  .article-wrap ol, .article-wrap ul { margin: 0 0 16px; padding-left: 22px; color: #374151; line-height: 1.8; }
  .article-wrap li { margin-bottom: 6px; }
  .article-wrap code { padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 5px; background: #eef2f7; color: #0f172a; font: 600 .88em ui-monospace, Consolas, monospace; }
  .article-wrap pre { margin: 0 0 20px; padding: 17px 19px; border-radius: 12px; overflow-x: auto; background: #0d1524; color: #dce8f7; }
  .article-wrap pre code { padding: 0; background: none; color: inherit; font: 500 .87rem/1.7 ui-monospace, Consolas, monospace; }

  .related-tools { margin-top: 54px; padding: 26px; border: 1px solid #dbe4f0; border-radius: 16px; background: #f6f9fd; }
  .related-tools h2 { margin: 0 0 6px; }
  .related-tools p { margin: 0; }
  .related-tools ul { list-style: none; margin: 16px 0 0; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 10px; }
  .related-tools li { margin: 0; }
  .related-tools a { display: block; padding: 12px 14px; border: 1px solid #cbd7e7; border-radius: 10px; background: #fff; color: #1d4ed8; font-weight: 800; font-size: .9rem; text-decoration: none; }
  .related-tools a:hover { border-color: #2563eb; background: #eff6ff; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hero { padding: 44px 0 36px; }
  }
</style>
